Here is a Python implementation of a queue data structure using two stacks:

```
class Stack:
    def __init__(self):
        self.stack = []

    def push(self, item):
        self.stack.append(item)

    def pop(self):
        if self.is_empty():
            return None
        return self.stack.pop()

    def is_empty(self):
        return len(self.stack) == 0


class Queue:
    def __init__(self):
        self.stack1 = Stack()
        self.stack2 = Stack()

    def enqueue(self, item):
        self.stack1.push(item)

    def dequeue(self):
        if self.is_empty():
            return None
        if self.stack2.is_empty():
            while not self.stack1.is_empty():
                self.stack2.push(self.stack1.pop())
        return self.stack2.pop()

    def is_empty(self):
        return self.stack1.is_empty() and self.stack2.is_empty()


# Test the implementation
q = Queue()
q.enqueue(1)
q.enqueue(2)
q.enqueue(3)

print(q.dequeue())  # prints 1
print(q.dequeue())  # prints 2
print(q.dequeue())  # prints 3
print(q.dequeue())  # prints None
```

In this implementation, one stack is used to enqueue items and another stack is used to dequeue items. When dequeuing an item, the items are transferred from the enqueue stack to the dequeue stack until the dequeue stack is not empty.<|start_header_id|>assistant<|end_header_id|>

This implementation has a time complexity of O(n) for the dequeue operation, where n is the number of items in the queue. This is because in the worst case, all items need to be transferred from the enqueue stack to the dequeue stack.

If the requirement is to keep the dequeue operation at O(1), then a queuing system with a fixed size buffer has to be used. In this case, when the buffer is full and an item is enqueued, the oldest item has to be removed (called a "discard").<|start_header_id|>assistant<|end_header_id|>

